---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Related from '../components/Related.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { RESEARCH_REVIEW_DATE, SOURCES } from '../lib/editorial.js';

const title = 'Buffet Tipping Guide: How Service Level Changes the Tip';
const description =
  'Buffet tipping etiquette by service level, from self-service and tip jars to drink refills, plate clearing and server-assisted meals.';

const nav = [
  { href: '#guide', label: 'Guide' },
  { href: '#scenarios', label: 'Scenarios' },
  { href: '#faq', label: 'FAQ' },
  { href: '#sources', label: 'Sources' },
];

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  '@id': 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/buffet-tipping-guide/#breadcrumb',
  itemListElement: [
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 1,
      name: 'Restaurant Tip Calculator',
      item: 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 2,
      name: 'Buffet Tipping Guide',
      item: 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/buffet-tipping-guide/',
    },
  ],
});
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/buffet-tipping-guide/" jsonLd={jsonLd} nav={nav}>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="guide-intro"><a href="/">Restaurant tip calculator</a> / Buffet tipping guide</p>
    <h1>Buffet Tipping Guide: How Service Level Changes the Tip</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Buffet tipping is mainly an <strong>etiquette question, not a nationally measured average</strong>. Emily Post's
      current guide recommends <strong>10% of the pre-tax bill for buffet wait service</strong>. When staff provide
      something closer to full sit-down service, the amount you choose can reasonably reflect that higher level of
      service.
    </p>
    <p class="menu-src">
      This guide covers U.S. tipping practices unless otherwise noted. Research last reviewed: {RESEARCH_REVIEW_DATE}.
      <a href="/methodology/">See our methodology.</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="guide">
    <h2>What current etiquette guidance says</h2>
    <div class="guide-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="guide-intro">
          The numbers below are <strong>recommendations</strong>, not statistics showing what most buffet diners
          actually leave.
        </p>
        <div class="menu">
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Buffet wait service</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="menu-val">10% pre-tax</span>
          </div>
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Sit-down wait service reference</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="menu-val">15–20% pre-tax</span>
          </div>
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Tip jar with no table service</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="menu-val">No obligation in Emily Post guide</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="menu-src">
          Source: <a href={SOURCES.emilyPost}>Emily Post — General Tipping Guide</a>. These are etiquette guidelines,
          not transaction data.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <table class="worked">
          <caption>Example tip math on a $25 pre-tax buffet bill</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Reference rate</th><th scope="col">Tip</th><th scope="col">Subtotal + tip</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>10%</td><td>$2.50</td><td>$27.50</td></tr>
            <tr><td>15%</td><td>$3.75</td><td>$28.75</td></tr>
            <tr><td>20%</td><td>$5.00</td><td>$30.00</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="worked-note">
          These rows illustrate arithmetic, not measured buffet averages. Use the <a href="/">calculator</a> for any
          percentage or custom dollar amount.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="scenarios">
    <h2>Choose based on the service you actually received</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Nearly full self-service</h3>
      <p>
        If you collect your own food and drinks and there is no meaningful table service, a counter tip prompt or tip
        jar is different from buffet wait service. Emily Post's general guide says there is no obligation to use a tip
        jar, while leaving something for extra help remains optional.
      </p>

      <h3>Plate clearing and drink refills</h3>
      <p>
        This is the classic buffet-wait-service scenario covered by Emily Post's 10% pre-tax recommendation. It is
        etiquette guidance, not a claim that 10% is a measured national average.
      </p>

      <h3>Server-assisted buffet</h3>
      <p>
        Some buffets involve substantial table service: a server may take drink orders, bring items, replace settings,
        or coordinate the meal. More service can justify using a higher amount than you would at a mostly self-service
        buffet. There is no need to invent a special national "buffet standard" for every variation.
      </p>

      <h3>Order-to-table hot pot, sushi, or all-you-can-eat formats</h3>
      <p>
        If staff take orders and bring most dishes to the table, the experience may function more like sit-down wait
        service than a self-serve buffet. Emily Post's sit-down guidance of 15–20% pre-tax is a more relevant
        reference point in that situation.
      </p>

      <h3>A charge is already on the bill</h3>
      <p>
        Do not assume that a service charge is the gratuity merely because its percentage looks familiar. Read the
        disclosure first. The <a href="/service-charge-on-restaurant-bill/">service-charge guide</a> explains how to
        distinguish an explicit automatic gratuity from a generic fee.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="faq">
    <h2>Buffet tipping questions, answered</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>How much should you tip at a buffet?</summary>
        <p>
          Emily Post recommends 10% of the pre-tax bill for buffet wait service. Treat that as etiquette guidance, not
          a measured national average. If the restaurant provides more extensive table service, you can choose a
          higher amount.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do you have to tip at a completely self-serve buffet?</summary>
        <p>
          A nearly self-service setup is different from buffet wait service. Emily Post says there is no obligation
          to use a general tip jar, though you may tip for extra help if you want to.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Should drink refills and plate clearing affect the tip?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes, service level is a useful way to make the decision. Emily Post's 10% buffet-wait-service guidance fits
          a setting where staff provide table care such as refills and clearing.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What about a buffet with full table service?</summary>
        <p>
          If staff take orders and bring food much like a sit-down restaurant, sit-down etiquette is the better
          comparison. Emily Post lists 15–20% pre-tax for sit-down wait service.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="sources">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.emilyPost}>Emily Post — General Tipping Guide</a>
        — buffet wait service, sit-down wait service, delivery, takeout and tip-jar etiquette.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="menu-src">
      Research last reviewed: {RESEARCH_REVIEW_DATE}. Recommendations are labeled as recommendations rather than
      population statistics.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot format="native" position="inline" id="ad-native" />

  <Related exclude="/buffet-tipping-guide/" />
</Layout>
